  "answer": "Possibly, under the Manual’s practice—but not if the change would extend an already clearly defined claim. I cannot determine whether your particular amendment would be allowed.\n\n• Where the filed representation includes words alongside the shape or colour, the Manual says it may be appropriate to amend the description to identify all the elements as a combination, rather than claiming the parts independently. [TMM/Part21/2/2/3]\n\n• If the existing description is broad, vague or absent, adding that clarification does not necessarily extend your rights, but requires careful consideration. [TMM/Part21/2/2/3]\n\n• If the existing description already clearly specifies the scope of the mark, the Manual says the claim cannot be extended to include items outside that description. Words appearing in the representation are therefore not, by themselves, enough to justify adding them to a clearly restricted claim. [TMM/Part21/2/2/3]\n\nFor shape marks, the Manual also says there should be no discrepancy between the representation and description. [TMM/Part10/3/3/2/2]\n\nThese statements describe Manual practice, not legislation. No Act or Regulations passages were supplied to establish the legislative position."
